The 4 October export of ticket #4471 combines reports that the desk's code assigns to distinct causes. Counts below use unique report IDs and tenant locale, form version and channel, not the browser-based intake tag. They cover this export only.

## Separate problems

The shared parser reads dates month first even for day-first tenants. Separately, booking-v3 tells users to enter month first while the form reads the tenant's day-first format. Fixing one does not establish that the other is fixed.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The desk flagged reports that read as a one-day move. Their classification above follows tenant and channel, not a confirmed investigation of each report. Review those reports individually, including time zones and event timestamps, before assuming a shared cause or moving counts.

## Queue history

The chart separates arrival from merging. The large merge does not indicate when the incident began. The export is a snapshot, so follow the current queue for later reports.

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Triage by cause

The old subject-only decision is accepted and remains as history. The comparison below illustrates why a matching subject is insufficient: use tenant, form, channel and evidence of the actual failure; keep uncertain reports open for investigation. Different subjects can describe the same cause.

